## 3. Creating & Configuring User Criteria

### 1.Navigate to User Criteria Module:Access User Criteria table.

### Go to Service Catalog > Catalog Administration > User Criteria and click New.

### 2.Set Core Identification:Define entitlement record identification.

Provide a clear, standard Name (e.g., US Employees - IT Department or Executive Management Group).

### 3.Define Criteria Fields:Configure static user conditions.

Fill in standard user record criteria using the built-in field pickers:

### Users: Specific individual users (sys_user).

### Groups: User groups (sys_user_group).

### Roles: Required security roles (sys_user_role).

### Departments: Target organizational departments (cmn_department).

### Locations: Target geographical locations (cmn_location).

### Companies: Target corporate entities (core_company).

### 4.Configure Scripted Criteria (Advanced):Optional dynamic validation.

Check the Advanced checkbox to write custom JavaScript (user object is available automatically) for complex evaluations.

### 5.Associate to Catalog Records:Attach to items or categories.

Open your target Catalog Item or Category record, navigate to the Available For or Not Available For related list, and attach the User Criteria record.

### 4. Scripted User Criteria (Advanced Configuration)

When standard field matching (Location, Role, Group) is insufficient, check Advanced on the User Criteria form to execute custom logic.

### Script Context Variables

user: The sys_id of the currently logged-in user evaluating the catalog item.

user_id: Alias reference to the logged-in user record.

### Example: Checking User Custom Conditions via Script

JavaScript

/**

* Advanced User Criteria Script

* Evaluates whether a user has been active for more than 90 days

* @return {boolean} true to grant access, false to deny access

*/

checkUserEligibility();

function checkUserEligibility() {

```javascript
var userGr = new GlideRecord('sys_user');
```

if (userGr.get(user)) {

// Check custom field or logic (e.g., employment type and creation date)

var empType = userGr.getValue('employee_type');

var sysCreated = new GlideDateTime(userGr.getValue('sys_created_on'));

var now = new GlideDateTime();

```javascript
var diffSeconds = gs.dateDiff(sysCreated.getDisplayValue(), now.getDisplayValue(), true);
```

var diffDays = diffSeconds / 86400;

// Allow access only to Full-time staff employed for > 90 days

if (empType == 'full_time' && diffDays >= 90) {

return true;

}

}

return false;

}

### 5. Category-Level vs. Item-Level Inherited Entitlements

User Criteria can be applied at both the Category level and the Catalog Item level:

Category Inheritance: If a user does not have access to a Category via User Criteria, all items inside that category are automatically hidden from them.

Item-Level Override: If a user can see a Category, but a specific item inside has a restricting Not Available For rule, only that single item is hidden while the rest of the category remains visible.

